A||||12||DiggityDan||1999 3.4 Grand Am||||1||
Z||000000||DiggityDan||06-29-2000||08:15 PM||||A friend of mine had to get a rental car the other day while his car was being worked on. The car he got was a pretty sporty 99 Grand Am. But this car really impressed me, smooth running and yet had lots of power all the way to 6 grand. Curious, I popped the hood only to see a 3.4 sitting there. I like this engine, everything just seems to be in the right place for the swap to a fiero.And since my 2.8 threw a rod, I am looking at the difference of building it up or this 3.4. <P>I want to know if anyone has done this swap before. I can get my hands on a low mileage 99 and I don't think it would take too much work other than a few minor changes.<P>My major question is that I want to use the distributorless ignition that the 3.4 uses. How big of a pain in the ass is this to do, I understand that some people have switched their 2.8's over so it shouldn't be that big of a deal.<P>If you have anykind of information on this, please let me know, or if you know anyone that has used this motor in their fiero, let me know. I really want to talk to someone about this soon.<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Megafiero||06-29-2000||09:37 PM||||As for the 3.4 swap,  there would hardly be any work.  The 3.4L is actually the same block as a 2.8 only it has a longer stroke<BR>(same as a 3.1L) and has larger cylinders (instead of 3.5" their 3.62")  As for the distrubuter question   can't help ya.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-29-2000||09:41 PM||||Well bless your heart. I posted a topic some time ago saying this should be a good swap. I haven't actually heard of anyone doing it yet. To swap in the complete engine requires using the Grand Am ECM and wiring harness. Sorting out the wiring and electronics might just be the hardest (most time consuming) part of the whole swap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DiggityDan||06-29-2000||10:40 PM||||Batboy....I feel the exact same way. The wiring is where I would have a problem. I wouldn't know where to start splicing the harnesses together. I love this engine though. I think right now I may just swap in the block with my intake and distibutor, keep the computer and harness on the shelf until I have some time or find someone who can do this for me. <BR>Why even worry about trying to rig a camaro motor, the starter, alternator, A/C, and even the cooling system are exactly where they should be. My main question is the mounts, can you just use the fiero mounts like on the camaro 3.4? Do you have to modify them? <BR>Well Batboy, you talked me into it. I am going to pick up the motor and make this swap. I got someone going to give me a great deal on a motor with only 14,000 miles. <BR>Thanks for talking me into it Batboy.<BR>I let everyone know how it goes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||06-29-2000||10:53 PM||||If this 3.4 has the aluminum heads, you are in for a lot more work than you first planned. None of the fiero's intake or exhaust will fit the aluminum heads. The pistons are different, so you can't use the Fiero's heads without swapping to an iron head piston also. If the engine has iron heads- you are in great shape! Let us know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||06-30-2000||12:00 AM||||Swapping in the whole engine with electronics shuld be no more complicated that any 3800 swap, since those engines also use DIS. The 3400 has been of particular interest to me since the series I race in requires that you stay with an engine with the same bore centers as OEM, so the 3800, V8s etc are out. There are 3400's out there putting out almost 240 hp, the roller cam gives a nice wide power band, and it shouldn't be too hard to adapt the rotrex supercharger kit to take it up near 300 hp. Just as a bonus, since it's the basicly the same engine as the 2.8 you wot' have to contend with space hassles and the aluminum heads will save a few pounds too! Thumbs up for this swap!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||06-30-2000||02:14 AM||||I have heard that the block is already set-up for a distributor. All you have to do is use the Fiero Intake and possible a camshaft.<BR>This is unconfirmed. I do know the long block you can buy from GM has everything set up for the distributor.||||reg||4
Z||000007||DiggityDan||06-30-2000||03:08 AM||||The motor in my friends rental had aluminum heads, I haven't looked at the one that's for sale yet but I am sure it has aluminum heads too. I really don't care if it looks stock or not, most people don't even know what a fiero is much less what engine it has in it. The only people that would know a difference would be people that have an interest in these cars and it wouldn't bother me in the least if they knew I had installed a late model 3400 in place of the 2.8.<P>This will just give me all the more reason to get the install done right the first time. Not that big of an issue in my opinion.<P>Oreif..the motor I looked at ahad a freeze plug where the distributor goes. I would think it would accept a distributor, I think it even had the hold down hole tapped, not for sure though.<BR>As of right now, consider this 3400 in my car, minus a few details like I don't have the engine yet, but just imagine it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-30-2000||09:16 AM||||I think you have to redrill and move the starter.  Ed Parks has done about a million of 3.4/3.5/3.6 swaps.  I think he has some info on his site.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000009||lou_dias||06-30-2000||09:27 AM||||The Fiero's intake will not bolt up to these heads.  Totally different design.  I think GM performace parts catalog has aluminum heads for the v6 60 degree but will not accept fuel injection.  You need the Edelbrock intake and a 4 barrel Holley carb.<P>I would just swap in the entire engine.  The SFI system (and alum headS) in that engine is a hell of alot more efficient at being economical and producing power than the MFI Fiero system.  While you're at it, put in 1.6 roller rockers for a little performance boost at the top end.<P>I have a 3.5 block in my Fiero with all sorts of performance parts and an 89 3.1 cavalier Z24 with nothing done to it but maybe an aftermarket muffler still takes me.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||06-30-2000||10:00 AM||||Lou's right, the aluminum heads use a totally different intake manifold. These are not interchangeable with the intake manifolds from the cast iron head 60 degree V6 engines. Swapping in the entire 3.4 engine, including donor ECM and wiring, is the best way to go. I'm not aware of a carb manifold that will fit, but if there is one available, that's another option. The last option would be to use only the short block and install everything else from the Fiero, like heads manifolds, accessories, etc. You still have to change pistons though, because the aluminum head engines use domed pistons that won't work with the cast iron heads.<P>I don't know for sure, but I thought this was a FWD engine. Does anyone know for sure if the starter is on the right side of the motor? Why couldn't you use the whole engine and tranny from the Grand Am, it should be a 4 speed overdrive auto. That's what I would do, you have to swap the ECM anyway. If you use the tranny, it doesn't matter about the starter, because this engine and tranny was designed to fit together.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroshop||06-30-2000||10:26 AM||||Hey guys, there are two different 3.4 engines. The first is a very simple conversion and it is a Camaro/Firebird eng. that comes in the 94/95 models. That one is very easy to install. The second one with the aluminum heads is a different story. You need a new ECM, new exhaust and other items such as wiring etc.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||06-30-2000||01:34 PM||||Fieroshop is right. The 3.4 you want to swap is the overhead cam version. I have a friend doing it right now with an engine from a Monte Carlo. He is using the MC tranny and computer. Mounts needs to be fabricated as well as the exhaust. Its not an easy swap. Maybe like the 3.8 but that one is very popular and there are plenty of suppliers for prefabricated mounts, etc. Not the same for this 3.4.||||reg||1
